            ":step/content": {
              ":db/id": 17592186173161,
              ":tutorial/content": "Let's plot the slope field of the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}y} {\\textrm{d}x}=x-y.\n$$\n\nNotice that the right-hand side of our equation is a function of both $x$ and $y.$ However, the procedure for sketching the slope field is the same as usual.\n\nWe start by setting up a table of values.\n\n|  | $x=-1$ | $x=0$ | $x=1$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y=1$ |  |  |  |\n| $y=0$ |  |  |  |\n| $y=-1$ |  |  |  |\n\nWe then calculate the slope at each point and record each answer in the table. For example, the slope at the point $(0,1)$ is\n\n$$\n\\left.\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x}\\right|_{(0,1)} = 0-1 ={\\color{blue}{-1}}.\n$$\n\nFilling in our table gives the following set of results:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{|c|c|c|c|c|c|}\n\\hline\n\t&-1 \t&0 \t&1 \\\\\n\\hline\n -1\t&0 \t&1 \t&2\\\\\n\\hline\n0\t&-1 \t&0 \t&1\\\\\n\\hline\n1\t&-2 \t&{\\color{blue}{-1}} \t&0\\\\\n\\hline\n\\end{array}\n\\]\n\n|  | $x=-1$ | $x=0$ | $x=1$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y=1$ | $-2$ | ${\\color{blue}{-1}}$ | $0$ |\n| $y=0$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ |\n| $y=-1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ |\n\nFinally, sketching our slope field, we get the following diagram:\n\n![](images/2a/2a86de3bdb5716e2be58f02124ccfd7470c9d8eda50abdb01052200e9fecfa0d.png)\n\nWe're done!\n\nNote that if we were to pick more points and use a computer to plot our slope field, it would look more like this:\n\n![](images/42/42508880923b52e9a63b90514c01b33b68c45385cb1650e44832953a6111ee7b.png)\n\nFrom here, it's easy to get an idea of the solution curves. For example, three particular solutions to the differential equation are given below.\n\n![](images/76/7634837eabf2be5ceb2ac56d22576170238b2640181aadde2417e8ff7a2bbd83.png)",
              ":tutorial/id": "790a4898-1668-5214-a911-83977b559c34",
              ":tutorial/math-academy-id": 5050,
              ":tutorial/title": "Introduction"
            },
            ":step/id": "a89f8367-e99c-56cc-90c3-8399250144a7",
            ":step/math-academy-id": 31623,
            ":step/next": {
              ":db/id": 17592186172903
            }
          },
          {
            ":db/id": 17592186172907,
            ":step/content": {
              ":db/id": 17592186172041,
              ":knowledge-point/canonical-example": {
                ":db/id": 17592186171847,
                ":question/id": "ab9fe3df-7f60-55c5-b8af-810b0746993e",
                ":question/math-academy-id": "e-8576",
                ":question/problem": "Consider the differential equation $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}=(x+1)(y+1)$ and its slope field. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- At every point $(x,y)$ where $x \\gt -1$ and $y \\gt -1,$ the slope of the slope field is positive\n\n- At every point $(x,y)$ where $x \\lt -1$ and $y \\lt -1,$ the slope of the slope field is positive\n\n- At every point $(x,y)$ where $x \\gt -1$ and $y \\lt -1,$ the slope of the slope field is negative",
                ":question/worked-solution": "First, we define $f(x,y) = (x+1)(y+1).$ Then, our differential equation is $\\dfrac{\\textrm dy}{\\textrm d x} = f(x,y).$ \n\nNotice that $f(x,y) = 0$ when $x = -1$ or $y = -1.$ This divides the $xy$-plane into four regions, as shown below.\n\n![](images/e7/e7b398c7ff971e39b15980078232556d23866ab2f65a05b7d8df9a5f67b797dc.png)\n\nIf we compute the sign of $f(x,y)$ in each of those four regions (by considering some test points, for example), we get the following:\n\n![](images/94/943ed7c12c71438d567da5ec901435bae1d3c7e576ba9b6c933d9fddc4e42b8f.png)\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement:\n\n- Statement I is true. If $x \\gt -1$ and $y \\gt -1,$ then according to our diagram, we have\n\n$$\n\\left.\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} \\right|_{(x,y)} =f(x,y)  \\gt     0.\n$$\n\nThis implies that the slope of the slope field is positive for any point $(x, y)$ in this region.\n\n- Statement II is true. If $x \\lt -1$ and $y \\lt -1,$ then according to our diagram, we have\n\n$$\n\\left.\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} \\right|_{(x,y)} =f(x,y)  \\gt     0.\n$$\n\nThis implies that the slope of the slope field is positive for any point $(x, y)$ in this region.\n\n- Statement III is true. If $x \\gt -1$ and $y \\lt -1,$ then according to our diagram, we have\n\n$$\n\\left.\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} \\right|_{(x,y)} =f(x,y)  \\lt     0.\n$$\n\nThis implies that the slope of the slope field is negative for any point $(x, y)$ in this region.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""
              },
              ":knowledge-point/id": "75710e99-e13c-54ba-9401-486bfec15284",
              ":knowledge-point/key-prerequisites": [
                {
                  ":db/id": 17592186048795
                }
              ],
              ":knowledge-point/title": "Identifying Rectangular Regions Where a Slope Field Is Positive or Negative"
            },
            ":step/id": "1b9a1add-dc4b-5533-80c6-2e82bf931b5c",
            ":step/math-academy-id": 31856,
            ":step/next": {
              ":db/id": 17592186172978
            }
          },
          {
            ":db/id": 17592186172978,
            ":step/content": {
              ":db/id": 17592186173194,
              ":tutorial/content": "Note the following facts regarding slope fields:\n\n- Recall that for a differential equation of the form $y' = f(x),$ the slope field has *vertical* symmetry.\n\n- Also recall that for a differential equation of the form $y' = g(y),$ the slope field has *horizontal* symmetry.\n\n- In contrast, a differential equation of the form $y' = f(x,y)$ usually has neither vertical nor horizontal symmetry.\n\nWe can use these facts to determine the type of equation that generates a particular slope field. Let's see an example.",
              ":tutorial/id": "03296d2b-05de-5077-8eab-c619925af706",
              ":tutorial/math-academy-id": 5668,
              ":tutorial/title": "Symmetry in Slope Fields"
            },
            ":step/id": "8cc2f789-24f5-5327-b95b-19f422496a28",
            ":step/math-academy-id": 34795,
            ":step/next": {
              ":db/id": 17592186172905
            }
          }
        ],
